1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,960
Every second, tens of billions
of particles pass through your thumbnail.

2
00:00:04,704 --> 00:00:06,364
Through your skin, your bones,

3
00:00:06,444 --> 00:00:08,804
and straight through the Earth
beneath your feet.

4
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:13,249
Through your whole body, it's about
a hundred trillion every second.

5
00:00:13,941 --> 00:00:16,361
They don't stop. They don't slow down.

6
00:00:16,441 --> 00:00:19,061
And almost none of them ever touch you.

7
00:00:19,608 --> 00:00:21,628
At night, they don't stop either.

8
00:00:22,088 --> 00:00:25,308
They come up through the floor,
after crossing the entire planet.

9
00:00:26,063 --> 00:00:31,163
Even your body gives off thousands every
second, from the potassium inside it.

10
00:00:31,663 --> 00:00:33,203
They're called neutrinos.

11
00:00:33,283 --> 00:00:37,263
And for a long time, nobody was sure
they existed at all.

12
00:00:37,699 --> 00:00:41,519
So how can something pass through
a whole planet without hitting anything?

13
00:00:42,219 --> 00:00:45,239
And if it never hits anything,
how do we know it's there?

14
00:00:47,649 --> 00:00:50,329
The story starts
with something that went missing.

15
00:00:50,851 --> 00:00:53,031
In 1914, James Chadwick

16
00:00:53,261 --> 00:00:56,791
was studying beta decay,
a kind of radioactivity

17
00:00:56,871 --> 00:00:59,531
where an atomic nucleus
throws out an electron.

18
00:01:00,355 --> 00:01:04,775
Every nucleus of the same kind
releases the same amount of energy.

19
00:01:05,535 --> 00:01:08,935
So every electron should come out
with the same energy, too.

20
00:01:09,784 --> 00:01:14,324
They didn't. Some came out fast,
some slow, spread across a whole range.

21
00:01:15,043 --> 00:01:18,783
On average, the electrons
carried away only part of the energy.

22
00:01:19,523 --> 00:01:21,043
The rest seemed to vanish.

23
00:01:21,997 --> 00:01:23,277
This was serious.

24
00:01:23,957 --> 00:01:28,197
Conservation of energy is one of the rules
that physics relies on most.

25
00:01:28,817 --> 00:01:31,237
Some physicists were ready to give it up.

26
00:01:32,382 --> 00:01:36,282
In 1930, Wolfgang Pauli
suggested something else.

27
00:01:37,102 --> 00:01:41,002
In a letter to a meeting of physicists,
he called it a desperate remedy.

28
00:01:41,453 --> 00:01:45,433
What if the nucleus released
a second particle alongside the electron?

29
00:01:46,213 --> 00:01:50,533
Neutral, very light, and invisible
to every instrument of the day.

30
00:01:50,951 --> 00:01:53,031
It would carry off the missing energy.

31
00:01:53,771 --> 00:01:57,931
The two particles would share it,
sometimes one way, sometimes the other.

32
00:01:58,411 --> 00:02:00,651
That's why the electrons never matched.

33
00:02:01,841 --> 00:02:03,441
Pauli called it the neutron.

34
00:02:04,321 --> 00:02:07,101
But in 1932, the same James Chadwick

35
00:02:07,331 --> 00:02:11,221
found a much heavier neutral particle,
and that one kept the name.

36
00:02:11,495 --> 00:02:14,615
So Enrico Fermi gave Pauli's particle
a new one.

37
00:02:15,475 --> 00:02:16,155
Neutrino.

38
00:02:16,385 --> 00:02:18,895
Italian for "little neutral one".

39
00:02:21,205 --> 00:02:22,785
But Pauli had a problem.

40
00:02:23,525 --> 00:02:27,465
A particle that slips through everything
also slips through every detector.

41
00:02:28,036 --> 00:02:31,216
The story goes that he bet
the astronomer Walter Baade

42
00:02:31,296 --> 00:02:34,396
a case of champagne
that nobody would ever catch one.

43
00:02:34,916 --> 00:02:38,276
For a quarter of a century,
it looked like a safe bet.

44
00:02:39,359 --> 00:02:41,019
Then two American physicists,

45
00:02:41,259 --> 00:02:45,039
Frederick Reines and Clyde Cowan,
turned the problem around.

46
00:02:45,899 --> 00:02:49,819
If neutrinos almost never interact,
use an enormous number of them.

47
00:02:50,137 --> 00:02:53,897
A nuclear reactor
produces huge numbers of antineutrinos,

48
00:02:54,137 --> 00:02:56,617
the antimatter twins of neutrinos.

49
00:02:56,997 --> 00:03:00,977
So they put tanks of water
next to a reactor in South Carolina.

50
00:03:01,755 --> 00:03:05,875
Once in a long while, an antineutrino
hits a proton in the water.

51
00:03:06,595 --> 00:03:10,355
The proton becomes a neutron,
and a positron flies off.

52
00:03:10,783 --> 00:03:15,123
The positron vanishes almost at once,
in a flash of two gamma rays.

53
00:03:15,529 --> 00:03:17,429
A few millionths of a second later,

54
00:03:17,509 --> 00:03:20,989
the neutron is swallowed
by cadmium dissolved in the water.

55
00:03:22,209 --> 00:03:23,409
Another flash.

56
00:03:23,976 --> 00:03:25,876
Flash, pause, flash.

57
00:03:26,396 --> 00:03:28,436
Almost nothing else makes that pattern.

58
00:03:29,687 --> 00:03:34,087
On the fourteenth of June, 1956,
they sent Pauli a telegram.

59
00:03:34,587 --> 00:03:36,467
They had found his particle.

60
00:03:37,209 --> 00:03:41,049
Pauli wrote back: "Everything comes to him
who knows how to wait."

61
00:03:41,964 --> 00:03:43,244
The reply never reached them.

62
00:03:43,474 --> 00:03:47,124
Reines only learned of it
many years later.

63
00:03:48,022 --> 00:03:53,162
And the champagne? According to the story,
Pauli paid up, and the theorists drank it.

64
00:03:53,842 --> 00:03:55,762
The two experimenters got none.

65
00:03:58,342 --> 00:04:01,982
So what is a neutrino,
and why does it pass through everything?

66
00:04:03,105 --> 00:04:05,065
It's a relative of the electron.

67
00:04:05,425 --> 00:04:09,965
But it has no electric charge,
so electric forces can't touch it.

68
00:04:10,177 --> 00:04:14,757
And it ignores the strong force,
the one that holds atomic nuclei together.

69
00:04:15,215 --> 00:04:17,615
That leaves just two ways to interact.

70
00:04:17,975 --> 00:04:21,415
Gravity, which is hopelessly weak
for a single particle.

71
00:04:22,075 --> 00:04:23,275
And the weak force.

72
00:04:23,955 --> 00:04:28,435
So it's not that atoms are mostly empty,
and neutrinos slip through the gaps.

73
00:04:28,955 --> 00:04:31,875
If that were the reason,
everything would slip through.

74
00:04:32,158 --> 00:04:35,798
It's that a neutrino can fly
right through an atomic nucleus,

75
00:04:36,098 --> 00:04:38,958
and the chance of anything happening
is almost zero.

76
00:04:40,214 --> 00:04:41,714
How close to zero?

77
00:04:42,234 --> 00:04:45,594
Send a neutrino from the Sun
straight through the middle of the Earth.

78
00:04:46,085 --> 00:04:49,505
Almost thirteen thousand kilometres
of rock and iron.

79
00:04:49,945 --> 00:04:53,105
Fewer than one in a billion
will hit anything on the way.

80
00:04:53,914 --> 00:04:55,414
So build a wall of lead.

81
00:04:55,494 --> 00:04:57,154
Not a metre thick.

82
00:04:57,234 --> 00:05:00,094
Not a kilometre. A light-year thick.

83
00:05:00,777 --> 00:05:03,877
That's nearly ten trillion kilometres
of solid lead.

84
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:05,680
Fire neutrinos from the Sun

85
00:05:05,910 --> 00:05:07,400
at that wall, and most of them

86
00:05:07,630 --> 00:05:09,220
would come out the other side.

87
00:05:11,620 --> 00:05:14,260
And the Sun is firing them at us
all the time.

88
00:05:14,631 --> 00:05:17,371
In its core, hydrogen fuses into helium.

89
00:05:17,791 --> 00:05:23,751
Every time four hydrogen nuclei become one
helium nucleus, two neutrinos are born.

90
00:05:24,393 --> 00:05:25,713
The Sun does this so often

91
00:05:25,943 --> 00:05:28,673
that it releases about
a hundred and eighty

92
00:05:28,903 --> 00:05:32,053
trillion trillion trillion neutrinos
every second.

93
00:05:32,502 --> 00:05:33,982
That sounds absurd.

94
00:05:34,762 --> 00:05:38,742
But the Sun gives off about
ten million times more particles of light.

95
00:05:39,302 --> 00:05:41,062
We just happen to see those.

96
00:05:41,997 --> 00:05:45,177
By the time they reach us,
that's sixty-five billion every second

97
00:05:45,407 --> 00:05:47,537
through every square centimetre.

98
00:05:48,197 --> 00:05:49,997
Your thumbnail, from the start.

99
00:05:51,092 --> 00:05:55,092
And they get out fast.
The energy that leaves the Sun as light

100
00:05:55,322 --> 00:05:58,992
spends tens of thousands of years
working its way up from the core.

101
00:05:59,374 --> 00:06:01,914
A neutrino leaves in about two seconds.

102
00:06:02,654 --> 00:06:05,054
Eight minutes later,
it's passing through you.

103
00:06:05,352 --> 00:06:06,592
So the sunlight you see today

104
00:06:06,822 --> 00:06:10,012
began its journey out of the core
long before the pyramids.

105
00:06:10,872 --> 00:06:14,132
A neutrino tells you what the core
was doing eight minutes ago.

106
00:06:16,572 --> 00:06:20,032
But how do you catch something
that a light-year of lead won't stop?

107
00:06:20,815 --> 00:06:23,515
You make the target enormous.
And you wait.

108
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:28,750
In Japan, a kilometre under a mountain,
sits Super-Kamiokande.

109
00:06:29,260 --> 00:06:32,960
A tank holding fifty thousand tonnes
of ultra-pure water.

110
00:06:33,497 --> 00:06:37,057
Its walls are lined with
more than eleven thousand light sensors,

111
00:06:37,297 --> 00:06:38,997
each half a metre across.

112
00:06:39,737 --> 00:06:43,557
Very rarely, a neutrino
slams into an electron in the water,

113
00:06:43,697 --> 00:06:47,757
hard enough to send it racing
faster than light travels through water.

114
00:06:48,057 --> 00:06:50,857
It leaves a faint ring of blue light.

115
00:06:51,704 --> 00:06:53,584
Every day, about a hundred thousand

116
00:06:53,664 --> 00:06:57,084
billion billion solar neutrinos
pass through that tank.

117
00:06:58,131 --> 00:07:00,031
It catches around fifteen.

118
00:07:02,142 --> 00:07:05,242
Long before Super-Kamiokande,
someone tried to count

119
00:07:05,472 --> 00:07:08,162
the Sun's neutrinos
with a much stranger tank.

120
00:07:08,630 --> 00:07:12,990
In the 1960s, Ray Davis
built it almost a mile underground,

121
00:07:13,310 --> 00:07:15,510
in a gold mine in South Dakota.

122
00:07:16,078 --> 00:07:20,718
He filled it with three hundred and eighty
thousand litres of dry-cleaning fluid.

123
00:07:21,465 --> 00:07:23,225
The fluid is full of chlorine.

124
00:07:23,565 --> 00:07:28,805
Every so often, a neutrino from the Sun
turns one chlorine atom into argon.

125
00:07:29,328 --> 00:07:33,128
Every couple of months, Davis
flushed the argon out and counted it.

126
00:07:33,688 --> 00:07:36,568
Not grams of it. Individual atoms.

127
00:07:37,691 --> 00:07:40,511
He found about one new atom
every two days.

128
00:07:41,622 --> 00:07:43,162
And that was the problem.

129
00:07:43,822 --> 00:07:47,062
The best models of the Sun
predicted three times as many.

130
00:07:47,579 --> 00:07:50,679
Two thirds of the Sun's neutrinos
seemed to be missing.

131
00:07:51,965 --> 00:07:55,765
Either the experiment was wrong,
or our picture of the Sun was wrong.

132
00:07:56,325 --> 00:07:59,245
For more than thirty years,
nobody could say which.

133
00:08:01,675 --> 00:08:05,155
It was the second time something
had gone missing around neutrinos.

134
00:08:05,835 --> 00:08:07,915
And this time, the answer was stranger.

135
00:08:09,825 --> 00:08:11,885
Neutrinos come in three kinds,
or flavours.

136
00:08:11,965 --> 00:08:14,325
Electron neutrinos.

137
00:08:14,555 --> 00:08:17,865
Muon neutrinos. Tau neutrinos.

138
00:08:18,131 --> 00:08:20,231
What sets them apart is their partner.

139
00:08:21,151 --> 00:08:24,711
When a neutrino does hit something,
it can turn into an electron,

140
00:08:24,791 --> 00:08:28,391
a muon or a tau,
the electron's two heavier cousins.

141
00:08:29,195 --> 00:08:31,695
The Sun makes only electron neutrinos.

142
00:08:31,775 --> 00:08:35,635
And Davis's tank could only catch
electron neutrinos.

143
00:08:36,233 --> 00:08:39,573
But on the way to Earth,
a neutrino doesn't keep its flavour.

144
00:08:40,433 --> 00:08:43,473
It switches between them,
back and forth, as it travels.

145
00:08:44,067 --> 00:08:46,647
By the time they arrived,
many had turned into flavours

146
00:08:46,877 --> 00:08:48,847
the tank simply couldn't see.

147
00:08:50,032 --> 00:08:54,152
By 2002, a detector in Canada
that could see every flavour

148
00:08:54,382 --> 00:08:56,752
counted all the neutrinos from the Sun.

149
00:08:57,560 --> 00:08:59,940
None were missing. They had just changed.

150
00:09:02,551 --> 00:09:04,211
And that has a consequence.

151
00:09:04,911 --> 00:09:07,771
A particle can only switch
like this if it has mass.

152
00:09:08,337 --> 00:09:12,337
For decades, the simplest theory
had treated neutrinos as massless.

153
00:09:12,517 --> 00:09:13,977
They aren't.

154
00:09:14,057 --> 00:09:17,457
That discovery won a Nobel Prize in 2015.

155
00:09:19,043 --> 00:09:22,543
Pauli thought his particle
might weigh about as much as an electron.

156
00:09:23,099 --> 00:09:25,239
Today, we know it's at least
a million times lighter.

157
00:09:25,469 --> 00:09:28,759
And we still don't know
exactly how much it weighs.

158
00:09:31,399 --> 00:09:34,699
A particle that almost never interacts
sounds useless.

159
00:09:35,339 --> 00:09:36,539
It's the opposite.

160
00:09:37,814 --> 00:09:39,854
On the twenty-third of February, 1987,

161
00:09:40,084 --> 00:09:43,794
in a neighbouring galaxy,
a giant star collapsed.

162
00:09:44,409 --> 00:09:47,749
Almost all the energy of the collapse
left as neutrinos.

163
00:09:48,149 --> 00:09:52,609
They streamed straight out of the star,
while its light was still trapped inside.

164
00:09:53,252 --> 00:09:55,392
And they probably did more than escape.

165
00:09:56,312 --> 00:09:57,972
Physicists think a small share of them

166
00:09:58,202 --> 00:10:01,832
was absorbed on the way out,
and that's what blew the star apart.

167
00:10:03,073 --> 00:10:05,673
After a hundred and sixty-eight
thousand years in space,

168
00:10:05,903 --> 00:10:11,313
twenty-five of them were caught in Japan,
the United States and the Soviet Union.

169
00:10:11,737 --> 00:10:13,517
All within thirteen seconds.

170
00:10:13,597 --> 00:10:16,917
Two to three hours
before its light reached us.

171
00:10:17,952 --> 00:10:20,592
Closer to home,
neutrinos from inside the Earth

172
00:10:20,822 --> 00:10:24,092
have shown that about half of its heat
comes from radioactivity.

173
00:10:26,932 --> 00:10:29,492
And there's one more population
of neutrinos.

174
00:10:30,232 --> 00:10:31,532
The oldest of all.

175
00:10:32,107 --> 00:10:34,327
About one second after the Big Bang,

176
00:10:34,407 --> 00:10:37,887
the universe thinned out enough
for neutrinos to stop interacting.

177
00:10:38,647 --> 00:10:40,967
They've been flying freely ever since.

178
00:10:41,656 --> 00:10:45,276
Just before they broke free, they helped
decide how many neutrons survived,

179
00:10:45,506 --> 00:10:48,796
and so how much helium the universe made.

180
00:10:49,256 --> 00:10:51,976
About a quarter of all ordinary matter.

181
00:10:52,698 --> 00:10:56,658
According to our best calculations,
every cubic centimetre of space

182
00:10:56,888 --> 00:10:59,898
holds about three hundred
and thirty-six of them.

183
00:11:00,189 --> 00:11:02,049
And the neutrinos from the Sun?

184
00:11:02,329 --> 00:11:05,769
Only about two
in that same cubic centimetre.

185
00:11:06,060 --> 00:11:07,560
The neutrinos from the first second

186
00:11:07,790 --> 00:11:09,980
of the universe outnumber them

187
00:11:10,210 --> 00:11:11,580
a hundred and fifty to one.

188
00:11:12,529 --> 00:11:15,949
We've seen their fingerprints
in the oldest light in the universe.

189
00:11:16,609 --> 00:11:18,949
But no detector has ever caught one.

190
00:11:19,189 --> 00:11:21,549
They carry far too little energy.

191
00:11:22,293 --> 00:11:24,533
So will space keep filling up with them?

192
00:11:25,453 --> 00:11:25,853
No.

193
00:11:26,083 --> 00:11:29,273
Every star that has ever shone
has added only a trickle.

194
00:11:29,945 --> 00:11:31,905
The first second of the universe made tens

195
00:11:32,135 --> 00:11:34,985
of billions of times more
than all the stars since.

196
00:11:35,431 --> 00:11:38,791
And as the universe expands,
they spread thinner and thinner.

197
00:11:39,511 --> 00:11:42,871
They've cooled to about two degrees
above absolute zero.

198
00:11:43,507 --> 00:11:47,967
But they still have mass.
They move too fast to settle into clumps,

199
00:11:48,047 --> 00:11:51,747
so even that tiny mass smooths out
the cosmic web, just slightly,

200
00:11:51,827 --> 00:11:54,407
and that's how astronomers
are trying to weigh them.

201
00:11:56,957 --> 00:12:00,597
So right now, while you watch this,
tens of billions of neutrinos

202
00:12:00,827 --> 00:12:03,137
from the Sun
are passing through your thumbnail.

203
00:12:03,616 --> 00:12:06,076
And all around you,
neutrinos from the first second

204
00:12:06,306 --> 00:12:09,616
of the universe are drifting by,
almost without a trace.

205
00:12:11,536 --> 00:12:13,696
And yet, a particle
that barely touches anything

206
00:12:13,926 --> 00:12:17,596
has let us see inside the Sun,
inside an exploding star,

207
00:12:17,826 --> 00:12:20,496
and back to the first second
of the universe.

208
00:12:23,386 --> 00:12:25,266
So here's a question for you.

209
00:12:25,786 --> 00:12:27,586
Did it surprise you
just how many neutrinos

210
00:12:27,816 --> 00:12:29,726
are passing through you right now?

211
00:12:30,206 --> 00:12:31,476
Let me know in the comments.
